## Development

```bash
uv sync
uv run {{ package_name }} --help
uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check . && uv run ty check && uv run pytest
scripts/install_hooks.sh  # pre-commit warnings: stale --help docstrings, missing changelog fragment
```

`pytest` fails below the coverage floor in `pyproject.toml`; `ruff` includes
the security (`S`) rules; CI additionally scans `uv.lock` for known
vulnerabilities (OSV-Scanner) — the same as `osv-scanner --lockfile=uv.lock`
locally.

## Release

The version is the git tag (`vX.Y.Z`) — `pyproject.toml` has no `version =`.
Each change adds a changelog fragment instead of editing `CHANGELOG.md`:

```bash
uv run towncrier create +<slug>.<type>.md -c "What changed, for a human reader."
uv run scripts/release.py patch   # or minor / major (SemVer; at 0.x minor = breaking)
git push --follow-tags
```

`release.py` refuses off `main`, on a dirty tree or behind the remote, runs
the full gate, assembles `CHANGELOG.md` from `changelog.d/`, commits
`Release vX.Y.Z` and tags it; pushing is left to you.

## Install

**On this machine, from a local checkout:**

```bash
uv tool install .
```

**On any other machine (any OS), once this repo is pushed to GitHub — no
PyPI publish needed:**

```bash
{% if repository_url -%}
uv tool install "git+{{ repository_url }}.git"
{%- else -%}
uv tool install "git+https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git"
{%- endif %}
```

Pin a release by appending `@vX.Y.Z` (a tag cut by `scripts/release.py`)
to the URL. This is also how an AI agent
bootstraps this CLI on a fresh machine/sandbox where it isn't installed
yet — see `src/{{ package_name }}/data/SKILL.md`.

**Private repo?** The command above is unchanged — `uv` runs git under the
hood, so it authenticates the same way `git clone` does. On a machine
where the install is refused with an auth error, do one of these once:

```bash
gh auth login          # interactive login also sets up git's credential helper
gh auth setup-git      # only needed if gh was logged in with --with-token / in CI
```

or use the SSH form instead of HTTPS:

```bash
{% if repository_url -%}
uv tool install "git+ssh://git@github.com/{{ repository_url | replace('https://github.com/', '') }}.git"
{%- else -%}
uv tool install "git+ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git"
{%- endif %}
```

**Update** an installed copy to the latest commit:

```bash
uv tool upgrade {{ package_name }}
```

After install, the command `{{ package_name }}` is available globally, on
macOS, Linux and Windows alike (see `architecture.md` in the cli-generator
plugin for why: no shelling out, no hardcoded POSIX paths).

To pick up later architecture changes from the `cli-generator` template
itself, run `copier update` in this repo (requires this repo and the
template to both be git repos; see the `cli-generator` plugin's README).

## Architecture

This project follows a fixed CLI architecture:

- `src/{{ package_name }}/commands/` — argument parsing only, no business logic.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/core/` — services, models, errors: the real logic.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/providers/` — external APIs/integrations.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/credentials/` — secrets via the OS keychain (`keyring`)
  and environment variables. Secrets never live in `.{{ package_name }}/config.toml`.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/output/` — human (`rich`) and `--json` rendering.

Run `{{ package_name }} doctor` to check environment/config/auth readiness, and
`{{ package_name }} --help` for the full command surface. Every command supports
`--json` for machine/agent consumption; errors are structured (`error.code`,
fixed exit codes, `error.action` telling you what to do next) and go to
stderr in human mode, so stdout is always safe to pipe.

A project can require a minimum CLI version in its `.{{ package_name }}/config.toml`
(`[cli] min_version = "..."`); an older install refuses with exit 6 and
`uv tool upgrade {{ package_name }}` fixes it.

See `src/{{ package_name }}/data/SKILL.md` for the agent-facing usage guide
bundled with this package — install it into an AI tool with
`{{ package_name }} skill install`, or get it as plain text with
`{{ package_name }} --ai` (works even without file access).
